WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.060
Authentication and enrolment are often discussed together, but they solve different problems.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:10.200
Authentication determines how a person proves an identity and signs in to the Moodle site.

3
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:17.900
Enrolment makes that identity a participant in a course and usually assigns a course role such as student, non-editing teacher, or teacher.

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:26.320
Confusing the two creates support tickets: a user may be able to log in but not see a course, or be enrolled but unable to authenticate.

5
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:32.920
Moodle LMS can support several account and authentication patterns, depending on enabled plugins and policy.

6
00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:46.940
Accounts may be created manually, uploaded in bulk, self-registered through email confirmation, synchronized from a directory, authenticated through OAuth-based providers, connected to an external database, or handled by another approved method.

7
00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:49.280
No method is automatically best.

8
00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:59.140
The right choice depends on who owns identity data, how quickly changes must appear, how duplicates are prevented, and what happens when the source system is wrong.

9
00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:03.500
Stable identifiers matter because learning records have memory.

10
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:14.800
Enrolments, grades, completion history, forum contributions, reports, certificates of completion, and integrations depend on connecting activity to the correct account over time.

11
00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:22.020
If an email address is used as a practical identifier but can change, the administrator needs a plan for updates.

12
00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:27.480
If a username is reused, the site may preserve history in a way the organization did not intend.

13
00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:31.120
The identity lifecycle includes more than account creation.

14
00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:38.180
New users need correct names, email addresses, profile fields, and authentication method assignments.

15
00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:44.840
Active users may move departments, change names, shift cohorts, or require accessibility support.

16
00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:54.940
Departed users may need suspension, unenrolment, anonymization, deletion, or retention, depending on policy and legal obligations.

17
00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.000
These actions are not interchangeable.

18
00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:01.620
Suspension limits access while preserving the account.

19
00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:04.040
Unenrolment changes course participation.

20
00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:08.140
Deletion affects the account record and must be governed carefully.

21
00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:10.900
Enrolment methods need the same discipline.

22
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:23.160
Manual enrolment, self-enrolment, cohort synchronization, external database enrolment, payment-related enrolment, and other plugin-based methods each create a different source of truth.

23
00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:35.420
Administrators should know who approves participation, how exceptions are handled, whether enrolments expire, which role is assigned, and what evidence exists when a learner disputes access.

24
00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.900
Strong identity administration feels boring when it works.

25
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:47.800
Users arrive with the right access, course teams know where enrolments come from, departed users lose access appropriately, and reports remain coherent.

26
00:02:47.900 --> 00:03:01.340
That outcome depends on documented ownership, stable identifiers, least privilege, review routines, and a support process that treats identity problems as operational signals instead of isolated help-desk noise.
